    "font": "MyriadPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 10690613,
    "text": "•\t\nYou learn about recursion. Recursion is a coding \ntechnique used in many algorithms. It’s a building \nblock for understanding later chapters in this book.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     107.75700378417969,
     279.531982421875,
     355.00067138671875,
     320.7599792480469
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 10690613,
    "text": "•\t\nYou learn what a base case and a recursive case is. \nThe divide-and-conquer strategy (chapter 4) uses \nthis simple concept to solve hard problems.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     293.0110168457031,
     4.003999710083008,
     366.4185791015625,
     44.0
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Semibold",
    "size": 18.0,
    "color": 6513766,
    "text": "recursion",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     99.5,
     369.9549865722656,
     401.65472412109375,
     436.9449768066406
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "I’m excited about this chapter because it covers recursion, an elegant \nway to solve problems. Recursion is one of my favorite topics, but it’s \ndivisive. People either love it or hate it—or hate it until they learn to \nlove it a few years later. I personally was in that third camp. To make \nthings easier for you, I have some advice:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     99.5,
     444.1059875488281,
     408.660400390625,
     471.9469909667969
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "•\t This chapter has a lot of code examples. Run the code for yourself to \nsee how it works.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     99.5,
     479.1080017089844,
     414.9445495605469,
     545.9390258789062
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "•\t I’ll talk about recursive functions. At least once, step through a \nrecursive function with pen and paper: something like, “Let’s see, I \npass 5 into factorial, and then I return five times passing 4 into \nfactorial, which is . . . ,” and so on. Walking through a function like \nthis will teach you how a recursive function works.",
    "is_code": false
   }
  ],
