 },
 {
  "pdf_index": 73,
  "blocks": [
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.495986938476562,
     -3.6251630783081055,
     223.57000732421875,
     15.166749954223633
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Semibold",
    "size": 9.166999816894531,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Chapter 3  I  recursion\n48",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.495010375976562,
     35.11800765991211,
     330.9209289550781,
     88.95902252197266
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Every time you make a function call, your computer saves the values for \nall the variables for that call in memory like this. Next, you print hello, \nmaggie! Then you call greet2(\"maggie\"). Again, your computer \nallocates a box of memory for this function call.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.4959716796875,
     195.65798950195312,
     326.0635070800781,
     249.5010223388672
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Your computer is using a stack for these boxes. The second box is added \non top of the first one. You print how are you, maggie? Then you \nreturn from the function call. When this happens, the box on top of the \nstack gets popped off.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     352.1180114746094,
     327.9770202636719,
     470.958984375
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Now the topmost box on the stack is for the greet function, which \nmeans you returned to the greet function. When you called the greet2 \nfunction, the greet function was partially completed. This is the big \nidea behind this section: when you call a function from another function, \nthe calling function is paused in a partially completed state. All the values \nof the variables for that function are still stored on the call stack (i.e., in \nmemory). Now that you’re done with the greet2 function, you’re back \nto the greet function, and you pick up where you left off. First, you \nprint getting ready to say bye... Then you call the bye function.",
    "is_code": false
   }
  ],
  "images": [
   [
    62.750999450683594,
    101.69699096679688,
    272.27099609375,
    183.5369873046875
   ],
