   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     35.11800765991211,
     209.66653442382812,
     49.957008361816406
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "At this point, the call stack looks like this.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     189.93798828125,
     345.1195068359375,
     243.7830352783203
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "The “pile of boxes” is saved on the stack! This is a stack of half-\ncompleted function calls, each with its own half-complete list of boxes \nto look through. Using the stack is convenient because you don’t have to \nkeep track of a pile of boxes yourself—the stack does it for you.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     246.44503784179688,
     341.78753662109375,
     313.2920837402344
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Using the stack is convenient, but there’s a cost: saving all that info can \ntake up a lot of memory. Each of those function calls takes up some \nmemory, and when your stack is too tall, that means your computer is \nsaving information for many function calls. At that point, you have two \noptions:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     320.4530029296875,
     245.82464599609375,
     335.2920837402344
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "•\t You can rewrite your code to use a loop instead.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     342.30999755859375,
     319.7755126953125,
     383.2959899902344
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "•\t You can use something called tail recursion. That’s an advanced \nrecursion topic that is out of the scope of this book. It’s also only \nsupported by some languages, not all.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.639999389648438,
     397.04791259765625,
     88.73599243164062,
     411.7920227050781
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Bold",
    "size": 12.90736198425293,
    "color": 10690613,
    "text": "EXERCISE",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     415.4380187988281,
     337.1065368652344,
     469.2829895019531
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Bold",
    "size": 10.0,
    "color": 10690613,
    "text": "3.2  \u0007Suppose you accidentally write a recursive function that runs \nforever. As you saw, your computer allocates memory on the \nstack for each function call. What happens to the stack when your \nrecursive function runs forever?",
    "is_code": false
